{
  "dom_id": "step-q127970",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\nI. $¬P∨(P∧¬Q)$\n\nII. $¬P∨Q$\n\nIII. $¬P∨¬Q$",
  "worked_solution": "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\,¬P∨(P∧¬Q)\\,$ | $\\,¬P∨Q\\,$ | $\\,¬P∨¬Q\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬P∨(P∧¬Q)$ and $¬P∨¬Q$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
